  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA frost advisory remains in effect early this morning for\nBradford, Susquehanna, and Wyoming counties, where winds are\nlighter and skies have mostly cleared. Elsewhere, cloud cover\nremains across central NY with northwest flow off Lake America\nand lingering wind guests helping to mitigate frost formation.\nHigh pressure moves overhead today, bringing mostly sunny skies\nand northwest flow. Temperatures remain cool, with highs only\nreaching the low to upper 50s. Winds will be lighter than\nyesterday, generally under 10 knots with gusts up to 15 knots. \n\nA ridge axis begins to nudge into the region this evening and\novernight, allowing winds to shift southwesterly. Despite clear skies \nand lighter winds warm air advection should keep temperatures \nfrom falling near freezing. Cold air will remain entrenched for\none more night across areas mainly east of I-81, where widespread\nmid to upper 30s are expected. Farther west and into the \nWyoming Valley, continued warm air advection should keep \ntemperatures in the low 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nSouthwesterly flow continues to advect warmer air into the\nregion Wednesday and Thursday, with highs in the mid to upper\n60s and lows generally in the 40s. Otherwise, fairly quiet and\nmostly dry weather is expected through most of the week. A weak\nshortwave trough looks to move through Wednesday night into \nThursday, bringing a chance for rain showers, mainly across \nnorth central NY. A cold front associated with the \naforementioned through will bring cooler temperatures Friday and\nSaturday, with highs in the upper 50s to mid 60s, though \nconditions should remain dry. High pressure then builds back \ninto the region for the weekend, with temperatures rebounding \ninto the mid 60s to low 70s by Sunday.",
